## 🐳 Docker Patterns

### Dockerfile Template — `--frozen` dependency layer, `--locked` final sync
```dockerfile
FROM python:3.14-slim        # CORRECTION 28 — standard GIL build

# uv ships as a static binary; copy it from the official distroless image
COPY --from=ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:latest /uv /usr/local/bin/uv

WORKDIR /app

# Layer-cache dependencies separately from source.
# --frozen here: uv cannot check the lock until every pyproject.toml is in the image.
# (Workspace repos: use --no-install-workspace instead of --no-install-project.)
COPY pyproject.toml uv.lock ./
RUN uv sync --frozen --no-install-project --no-dev

# --locked here: everything is present now, so assert uv.lock matches pyproject.toml.
COPY . .
RUN uv sync --locked --no-dev

ENV PATH="/app/.venv/bin:$PATH"
EXPOSE 8501
CMD ["streamlit", "run", "app/main.py", "--server.port=8501", "--server.address=0.0.0.0"]
```

The final `--locked` is the point: it **fails the build if `uv.lock` is out of sync with
`pyproject.toml`** rather than silently re-resolving. The first layer uses `--frozen`
because the lock can't be checked yet; it still installs exactly the locked versions, and
the `--locked` sync after `COPY . .` validates them. That split is the pattern in uv's own
Docker guide (*Intermediate layers*), and it is what makes the image byte-reproducible and
the "deterministic build" claim defensible in an interview.
**Never `pip install` in a Dockerfile on this roadmap.**

